From 964b1088b48b990d7aceff1041c06aa266009c03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:39:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: the HDR state changes only with what the
 request carries

SetHDR() took the EOTF of a picture as the one of the output before it
looked at whether the display declares it, released the blob of the
metadata only where it made a new one, and did not look at whether
HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA was added to the request. The code is Kodi's own. So
GetEotf() said HLG for an HLG video on a display that declares PQ only,
while nothing was sent; the same with a display whose EDID, read again
when a video starts, no longer declares HDR, with a connector without the
property, and with a blob that the driver did not make. Three things
read it:
- the palette of the PGS subtitles of an HDR video is converted to sRGB
  unless the destination is HDR (ShouldConvertPQPaletteToSRGB()): on that
  SDR output they kept their PQ colours;
- a capture is tagged with it (CaptureMetadata);
- the depth of the HDR output is asked and logged for it ("HDR output:
  ...", and NV_OUTPUT_BPC with nvidia-drm), of an SDR output.
And where the property was not added, SetHDR() said that HDR goes out
(m_hdrBlob.IsValid()), so the renderer passed HDR through without its
signalling.

CHDRSignal (HDRSignal.h, pure) now holds the rule, and SetHDR() calls it
with the request:
- the metadata of a PQ or HLG picture is made and queued where the
  connector and the display take its EOTF; then, and only then, the
  EOTF, the blob and the value that a modeset queues again are the
  picture's, and what goes with HDR metadata (the stereo InfoFrame that
  ends, the choice of YCbCr 4:2:0) is queued with it;
- otherwise the output signals no HDR for that picture: metadata of
  another one that is still queued is taken away, with its blob; where
  none is, nothing is queued, so there is no modeset that changes nothing
  (Kodi made one where the driver did not make the blob);
- SetHDR(nullptr) takes the metadata away as always, also where none is
  known to be queued (Kodi's start);
- where not even that can be queued, the state stays the one before,
  since it is what the kernel keeps, and so does its blob, while its id
  may be queued again (Kodi released it before the new one was queued).
The blob of a new picture is made apart and replaces the one before only
once it is queued: no blob is alive but the one queued.

An HDR video that goes out without HDR says why, once ("HDR output: no
HDR signalling for this HLG video: the display does not declare this
transfer function in its EDID"); a line says when metadata of the video
before is taken away, and a warning when it could not be.

Whether the driver then accepts the commit that carries the request is
not decided here: a refused commit falls back to the request before it
(CDRMAtomic), as before.

A host test runs CHDRSignal::Request() with doubles of the request and of
the blob, and the same checks against Kodi's SetHDR() as it was,
transcribed on those doubles: they pass here and fail there. Seven
mutations of the header make it fail too.
